  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1226 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nAs low pressure continues to deepen in the central High Plains\nMonday, southeasterly winds will increase across southern\nWisconsin. Low pressure then ejects northeastward into the\nnorthern Plains, bringing widespread showers to western Wisconsin\nby Monday afternoon as WAA develops in all levels. With this\nstrong WAA, expect highs in the mid-60s. Instability may build\ninto the region briefly, bringing lightning potential. Severe\nstorms do not appear likely at this time, but clear skies in the\nafternoon may be enough to enhance instability to the point that\nstronger winds may become possible within storms that do develop.\n\nThe low continues to propagate northeastward along the western\nedges of high pressure centered over the Northeast U.S. Monday\nnight into Tuesday, keeping southern Wisconsin within the warm\nsector with continued gusty south winds. Temperatures may even\nreach 70 degrees on Tuesday simply through strong WAA. However,\nplenty of cloud cover and rain continues across the area. Areas\nthat see multiple rounds of storms may see local ponding of water\nand rises on area streams.\n\nWednesday, low pressure propagates into southern Ontario, bringing\na break in precipitation as its associated occluded front finally\nexits to the east. Wrap-around showers move back in by Wednesday \nnight as low pressure continues to progress northeastward. Winds\ndiminish and become westerly. High pressure building through the\nCanadian Prairies will likely bring an end to precipitation, but\nwith high pressure remaining mainly to the north of Wisconsin,\nmodeling indicates potential for shortwaves bringing additional\nprecipitation chances Thursday through Friday. High pressure\nfinally propagates into the Great Lakes Saturday, bringing an end\nto precipitation chances and highs back in the 50s.\n\nMH",
